# ============================================================================
# SIR: lodash.topath toPath
# A pure value -> property-path-array converter.
# This SIR is the BLIND contract. A re-emitter who has never seen the source
# MUST be able to reconstruct a behaviorally-equivalent toPath from this alone.
# Every claim below was VERIFIED by running lodash.topath@4.5.2.
# ============================================================================

UNIT toPath
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG toPath(value: any) -> Array            # single positional arg; returns a NEW array
DEPENDS-ON none                            # zero runtime deps; self-contained

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EMISSION CONTRACT (read first)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This unit is the DEFAULT export (CommonJS `module.exports = toPath`). The
# re-emitter MUST emit it as the module's default export:
#     export default function toPath(value) { ... }
# It is called as a single-argument function: toPath(value).
# It is PURE and READ-ONLY: no clock/RNG/env/fs/net seam. No injection needed.
#
# ONE internal-state caveat (does NOT make it non-deterministic for in-scope
# inputs, but the re-emitter must reproduce it — see node S6 / SCOPE):
# the string-parsing helper is MEMOIZED, and its cache compares keys with
# SameValueZero (===, plus NaN===NaN treated equal). Among JSON/codec-faithful
# inputs the ONLY pair that is `===`-equal yet would parse differently is
# +0 vs -0 — and -0 is excluded from scope (it does not round-trip the codec
# anyway). So for every in-scope input the output is a pure function of that
# single input. (Implementation detail: a fresh memoize cache per process is
# fine; results for distinct string keys are stable and order-independent.)

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEHAVIOR  (ordered; the top-level dispatch is a 3-way BRANCH)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node S0  DISPATCH on `value`:
#   BRANCH A  if value is a true Array (Array.isArray(value) === true)
#             -> "array-clone" path (node S1)
#   BRANCH B  else if value is a Symbol (typeof value === 'symbol', OR an
#             object whose Object#toString tag is '[object Symbol]')
#             -> return a one-element array holding the symbol UNCHANGED: [value]
#             (NOTE: the symbol is returned by identity, not stringified.)
#             # OUT-OF-ENVELOPE for genInputs: symbols are not codec-faithful.
#   BRANCH C  else (everything else: string, number, bigint, boolean, null,
#             undefined, plain object, ...)
#             -> "scalar string-parse" path (node S2)
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node S1  ARRAY-CLONE PATH  (BRANCH A)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Produce a NEW array of the same length. result[i] = toKey(value[i]) for
#   each index i in 0..length-1. (A shallow map; the input array is not
#   mutated and a new array object is returned.)
#   Empty array []  ->  [] (new empty array).
#
#   toKey(v)  (the per-element key normalizer):
#     - if typeof v === 'string'  -> return v unchanged (NOT parsed; the whole
#         string becomes ONE segment even if it contains '.' or '[' — e.g.
#         toKey('a.b') === 'a.b', so toPath(['a.b']) -> ['a.b']).
#     - if v is a Symbol          -> return v unchanged (identity).
#     - else                      -> let r = (v + '')   # JS string coercion
#                                     if r === '0' AND (1 / v) === -Infinity   # i.e. v is -0
#                                         return '-0'
#                                     else return r
#       JS string coercion `(v + '')` table (VERIFIED, array elements):
#           0        -> '0'        -0       -> '-0'   (the -0 guard fires)
#           42       -> '42'       1.5      -> '1.5'
#           1e21     -> '1e+21'    true     -> 'true'   false -> 'false'
#           null     -> 'null'     undefined-> 'undefined'
#           NaN      -> 'NaN'      Infinity -> 'Infinity'  -Infinity -> '-Infinity'
#           10n      -> '10'  (bigint)      [1,2]    -> '1,2'   (nested array joins)
#           {}       -> '[object Object]'
#           {toString:()=>'x.y'} -> 'x.y'   (custom toString respected; NOT path-parsed)
#     IMPORTANT: in the array branch, elements are NEVER run through the path
#     parser. They are only string/symbol-passed-through or coerced via toKey.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node S2  SCALAR STRING-PARSE PATH  (BRANCH C)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Step S2.1  s = toString(value)            # see node S3
#   Step S2.2  return stringToPath(s)          # see nodes S4..S5; returns NEW array
#   (Implementation returns a shallow copy of the parser's result array; treat
#    it as a fresh array each call.)
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node S3  toString(value)  (lodash _.toString — coerces to a string)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   if value === null OR value === undefined        -> '' (empty string)
#   else if typeof value === 'string'               -> value unchanged
#   else if value is a Symbol                        -> (handled earlier in S0;
#         not reachable here for symbols since BRANCH B intercepts them)
#   else  let r = (value + '')                       # JS string coercion
#         if r === '0' AND (1 / value) === -Infinity # value is -0
#              -> '-0'
#         else -> r
#   VERIFIED scalar coercions (the string that then enters the parser):
#       0   -> '0'      42 -> '42'    1.5 -> '1.5'   1e21 -> '1e+21'
#       true-> 'true'   false -> 'false'   NaN -> 'NaN'   Infinity -> 'Infinity'
#       null-> ''       undefined -> ''    (no-arg) -> ''
#       10n -> '10'     {} -> '[object Object]'   {toString:()=>'x.y'} -> 'x.y'
#   (Then S4/S5 PARSE that resulting string. So e.g. scalar 1.5 -> '1.5' ->
#    parsed -> ['1','5'] because '.' is a separator. See VERIFIED OUTPUTS.)
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node S4  stringToPath(s)  — overview
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Build result = [].
#   Step S4.1  s = toString(s)   # idempotent here; s is already a string
#   Step S4.2  LEADING-DOT rule: if s matches /^\./ (s starts with '.'),
#              push '' onto result FIRST.
#   Step S4.3  Scan s left-to-right with the global regex rePropName (node S5).
#              For EACH match m, in order, append exactly one segment computed
#              by node S5's append rule.
#   Step S4.4  return result.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node S5  rePropName — the property-name matcher (EXACT regex, load-bearing)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   reLeadingDot = /^\./
#   rePropName   = /[^.[\]]+|\[(?:(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?)|(["'])((?:(?!\2)[^\\]|\\.)*?)\2)\]|(?=(?:\.|\[\])(?:\.|\[\]|$))/g
#   reEscapeChar = /\\(\\)?/g
#
#   rePropName is one global regex with THREE top-level alternatives. The JS
#   replace callback receives (match, number, quote, qstr) where:
#       number = capture group 1 (the bracketed numeric index, if matched)
#       quote  = capture group 2 (the opening quote char ' or ", if matched)
#       qstr   = capture group 3 (the quoted body, if matched)
#   APPEND RULE for each match:
#       segment = qstr ? qstr.replace(reEscapeChar, '$1')
#                       : (number || match)
#       result.push(segment)
#   In words:
#     ALT-1  [^.[\]]+        a run of one or more chars that are none of
#                            '.', '[', ']'. -> push the matched text verbatim
#                            (as `match`; number & qstr are empty). This is the
#                            "bare property name" token. Spaces, '+', letters,
#                            digits, exponent-looking text, symbols, backslashes
#                            all fall here. e.g. 'foo bar' -> 'foo bar',
#                            '@#$' -> '@#$', '0e1' -> '0e1', '+1' -> '+1',
#                            '01' -> '01', '0x1' -> '0x1'.
#     ALT-2  \[ ( number | quoted ) \]   a bracketed segment, two sub-forms:
#              (a) NUMBER form: \[(-?\d+(?:\.\d+)?)\]  — an OPTIONAL leading '-',
#                  then >=1 digits, OPTIONALLY a '.' followed by >=1 digits.
#                  The captured digits (group 1) are pushed (no brackets).
#                  e.g. [0]->'0', [-1]->'-1', [1.5]->'1.5', [-0]->'-0'.
#                  This sub-form requires the WHOLE bracket body to be exactly a
#                  number. If it is not (e.g. [0e1], [1.], [+1], [01], [0x1],
#                  [ 0 ]), ALT-2 fails for the bracket and the inner chars are
#                  instead consumed by ALT-1 (the '[' and ']' are skipped, not
#                  emitted). e.g. a[0e1] -> ['a','0e1']; a[1.]  -> ['a','1']
#                  (the '1' matches ALT-2 number form '1', the lone '.' then is
#                  a separator); a[ 0 ] -> ['a',' 0 '] (ALT-1 keeps the spaces).
#              (b) QUOTED form: \[(["'])((?:(?!\2)[^\\]|\\.)*?)\2\]  — an opening
#                  quote (single or double), a (lazy) body, the SAME closing
#                  quote, all inside brackets. The body (group 3) allows any
#                  char that is neither the matching quote nor a backslash, OR a
#                  backslash-escaped char `\.` (any char after a backslash). The
#                  captured body then has ESCAPES UNWRAPPED via
#                  reEscapeChar.replace($1): /\\(\\)?/g  ->  replacement '$1'.
#                  Effect of the unwrap, VERIFIED:
#                     \"  (backslash + quote) -> "      # backslash dropped
#                     \\  (backslash + backslash) -> \  # collapses to one '\'
#                     \n  (backslash + 'n')      -> n   # backslash dropped, 'n' kept
#                     i.e. each '\' is removed; '\\' becomes a single '\'.
#                  e.g. a["x.y"].z -> ['a','x.y','z']  (dots inside quotes kept)
#                       a['x.y'].z -> ['a','x.y','z']
#                       a["x\"y"].z -> ['a','x"y','z']
#                       a["x\\y"].z -> ['a','x\\y','z']  (one backslash segment)
#                       a["x\ny"].z -> ['a','xny','z']
#                       a["a]b"]    -> ['a','a]b']   (']' fine inside quotes)
#                       a[""]       -> ['a','']      (empty quoted -> '')
#     ALT-3  (?=(?:\.|\[\])(?:\.|\[\]|$))   a ZERO-WIDTH lookahead that matches
#              the empty string at a position immediately FOLLOWED by either a
#              '.' or an empty-bracket '[]', which is in turn followed by '.',
#              '[]', or end-of-string. When this fires it pushes '' (since
#              match is the empty string, number/qstr empty -> push match '').
#              This is what produces the EMPTY segments for consecutive
#              separators and empty brackets:
#                   '.'   -> ['',''] (leading-dot rule pushes 1, lookahead 1)
#                   '..'  -> ['','','']
#                   'a..b'-> ['a','','b']
#                   'a.'  -> ['a','']
#                   'a[]' / 'a[].b' -> 'a' then '' (then 'b')
#                   '[]'  -> ['']
#                   'a[][]' -> ['a','','']
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node S6  MEMOIZATION (internal; reproduce but harmless for in-scope inputs)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   stringToPath is memoized on its FIRST argument (the value, BEFORE toString
#   in lodash's wiring — the memo key is the raw `value` passed to stringToPath,
#   which in the scalar path is the ORIGINAL `value`). Key equality is
#   SameValueZero: a === b, with NaN treated equal to NaN. Since +0 === -0,
#   scalar +0 and scalar -0 COLLIDE in the cache and the FIRST-seen of the two
#   determines the cached result for both within a process.
#   -> This is why -0 is OUT-OF-SCOPE (also non-round-trippable in JSON). For
#      every in-scope input no two distinct inputs collide, so behavior is a
#      pure function of the single input. A re-emitter may implement the parser
#      without a cache and still be behaviorally equivalent on all in-scope
#      inputs (the cache only ever returns the same value the parser would).

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VERIFIED OUTPUTS  (actual observed outputs from lodash.topath@4.5.2)
# These are the self-consistency anchors: genInputs emits these and the SIR
# above must reproduce every one.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   STRINGS (scalar path, parsed):
#     'a.b.c'            -> ['a','b','c']
#     'a'                -> ['a']
#     'a[0].b.c'         -> ['a','0','b','c']
#     'a[0].b'           -> ['a','0','b']
#     'a["x.y"].z'       -> ['a','x.y','z']
#     "a['x.y'].z"       -> ['a','x.y','z']
#     ''                 -> []                 # EMPTY STRING -> EMPTY ARRAY (not [''])
#     '.a'               -> ['','a']
#     '.a.b'             -> ['','a','b']
#     'a[0][1]'          -> ['a','0','1']
#     'a[0][1].b'        -> ['a','0','1','b']
#     '[0]'              -> ['0']
#     'a[-1]'            -> ['a','-1']
#     'a[1.5]'           -> ['a','1.5']
#     'a.'               -> ['a','']
#     'a..b'             -> ['a','','b']
#     '.'                -> ['','']
#     '..'               -> ['','','']
#     '...a'             -> ['','','','a']
#     'a...b'            -> ['a','','','b']
#     '[0][1][2]'        -> ['0','1','2']
#     'a[0].'            -> ['a','0','']
#     '["x"]["y"]'       -> ['x','y']
#     'a.0.b'            -> ['a','0','b']
#     'foo bar.baz'      -> ['foo bar','baz']
#     'a[""]'            -> ['a','']
#     'a[].b'            -> ['a','','b']
#     'a[][]'            -> ['a','','']
#     '[]'               -> ['']
#     '[].a'             -> ['','a']
#     '.[0]'             -> ['','0']
#     'a[0e1]'           -> ['a','0e1']
#     'a[0x1]'           -> ['a','0x1']
#     'a[+1]'            -> ['a','+1']
#     'a[01]'            -> ['a','01']
#     'a[ 0 ].b'         -> ['a',' 0 ','b']
#     'a[1.].b'          -> ['a','1','b']
#     'a[.5].b'          -> ['a','5','b']
#     'a["a]b"]'         -> ['a','a]b']
#     'a["a.b"]c'        -> ['a','a.b','c']
#     'a["x\"y"].z'      -> ['a','x"y','z']     # source string has escaped quote
#     'a["x\\y"].z'      -> ['a','x\\y','z']     # source has escaped backslash -> one '\'
#     'a["x\ny"].z'      -> ['a','xny','z']      # source has literal backslash+n -> 'n'
#     '@#$'              -> ['@#$']
#     '\\'               -> ['\\']               # single backslash string -> ['\\']
#     '\\.'              -> ['\\','']            # backslash literal, dot separator
#     'a\\.b'            -> ['a\\','b']
#   NUMBERS / BOOL / NULLISH / BIGINT (scalar path: toString THEN parse):
#     0                  -> ['0']
#     42                 -> ['42']
#     1.5                -> ['1','5']            # '1.5' -> parsed; '.' is a separator!
#     1e21               -> ['1e+21']            # '1e+21' has no '.','[',']' -> 1 token
#     true               -> ['true']
#     false              -> ['false']
#     NaN                -> ['NaN']
#     Infinity           -> ['Infinity']
#     null               -> []                   # toString -> '' -> []
#     undefined          -> []                   # toString -> '' -> []
#     (no argument)      -> []
#     10n  (bigint)      -> ['10']
#     123456789012345678901234567890n -> ['123456789012345678901234567890']
#     {}                 -> ['object Object']     # '[object Object]' parsed: '[' skipped,
#                                                 # 'object Object' is one ALT-1 token,
#                                                 # ']' skipped -> ['object Object']
#   ARRAYS (array-clone path: each element via toKey, NO parsing):
#     ['a','b']          -> ['a','b']
#     []                 -> []
#     [0,1]              -> ['0','1']
#     [-0]               -> ['-0']               # toKey -0 guard -> '-0' (array branch ok)
#     [null]             -> ['null']
#     [undefined]        -> ['undefined']        # OUT for genInputs (codec), but spec-defined
#     [true,false]       -> ['true','false']
#     [1.5]              -> ['1.5']              # NOT parsed: whole '1.5' is one segment
#     [{}]               -> ['[object Object]']  # NOT parsed
#     [['x']]            -> ['x']                # nested array (['x'] + '') -> 'x'
#     [[1,2],[3]]        -> ['1,2','3']
#     [NaN]              -> ['NaN']              # OUT for genInputs (codec); spec-defined
#     [Infinity,-Infinity] -> ['Infinity','-Infinity']  # OUT for genInputs (codec)
#     [1e21]             -> ['1e+21']
#     [10n,456n]         -> ['10','456']         # bigint elements; codec-faithful, IN scope
#     ['a.b']            -> ['a.b']              # KEY: array element strings are NOT path-parsed
#     [Symbol('k'),'a']  -> [Symbol('k'),'a']    # symbol kept by identity; OUT for genInputs

ORACLE value -> oracle.json

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCOPE
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
#   - the 3-way DISPATCH (array / symbol / scalar);
#   - toKey element normalization (string/symbol passthrough; else (v+'') with
#     the -0 guard);
#   - toString scalar coercion (null/undefined -> ''; else (v+'') with -0 guard);
#   - the full rePropName / reLeadingDot / reEscapeChar parsing pipeline,
#     including all three regex alternatives, the leading-dot rule, the number
#     vs quoted vs bare-token classification, the bracket-number strictness, the
#     escape unwrapping, and the empty-segment lookahead.
#   - returns a NEW array (the input array is never returned by identity and is
#     not mutated).
# CARRIED DATA: NONE. (No large literal table; the only constants are three
#   small regexes, transcribed verbatim above in node S5. No carried.json.)
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE for genInputs (codec or identity reasons —
#   the SIR still DEFINES the behavior so a blind re-emitter is complete, but
#   genInputs MUST NOT emit them because they would poison the held-out):
#   - SYMBOL values, top-level (-> [value]) and as array elements (-> kept by
#     identity): symbols are not JSON/codec-faithful and equality is by identity.
#   - scalar -0: (a) does not round-trip JSON.stringify (-0 -> 0), and (b) the
#     memoize cache makes +0/-0 collide and order-dependent. (Array element -0
#     ALSO does not round-trip JSON, so [-0] is OUT for genInputs even though
#     the array branch itself is deterministic; spec value documented above.)
#   - array elements that are undefined / NaN / +Infinity / -Infinity: not
#     codec-faithful as array members (JSON -> null). Spec values documented
#     above; genInputs excludes them.
#   - FUNCTIONS and objects with custom toString: behavior is defined (functions
#     coerce via (v+''); custom toString respected) but they are not stably
#     codec-faithful, so genInputs excludes them.
#   No stateful mutators, no side APIs, no ambient reads. Pure function.

